   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the administrative code of the city of New York, in relation to the general corporation tax",
    "basePrintNo": "S4898",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4898-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §11-1706, NYC Ad Cd",
    "lawSection": "New York City Administrative Code",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S4898\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the administrative code of the city of New York, in\nrelation to the general corporation tax\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis bill would extend the S-Corporation credit on the City's personal\nincome tax from July 1, 2015 through December 31, 2018. The credit is\nreceived by resident shareholders of S-Corporations that pay the New\nYork City general corporation tax (GCT) The credit is equal to 100\npercent of GCT liability for taxpayers with New York State taxable\nincome of $35,000 or less. The credit percentage decreases at higher\nincomes, and phases out to zero at a taxable income of $100,000.\n\nSTATEMENT IN SUPPORT:\n\nThis bill will enable New York City to continue providing relief to\nresident shareholders of S-Corporations that pay New York City's general\ncorporation tax.\n\nSmall businesses are the core of economic life in New York City. More\nthan three quarters of the firms in the City have less than 10 employ-\nees. These firms are found in all sectors of the City's economy-from law\nand finance to retailing and health care. Most firms of this size are\neither unincorporated or organized as S-Corporations. To be eligible for\nS-Corporation status, a corporation must have fewer than 100 stockhold-\ners, be owned by individuals rather than other corporations, and all\nowners must live in the United States.\n\n1 The Federal government has long offered these businesses an important\ntax break-- it has exempted them from the Federal corporate income tax.\nInstead, all income and deductions are passed on to the owners, where it\nis subject to their personal income tax, thus avoiding double taxation\non these earnings. Currently most states, including New York, New Jersey\nand Connecticut, recognize S-Corporations, and partially or fully exempt\nthem from their state corporate income taxes.\n\nIt is different in New York City. New York City residents that are\nstockholders in S-Corporations that do business in the City face double\ntaxation. First, their businesses pay the general corporation tax (GCT)\non the net earnings of the firm. These same earnings are passed on to\nthe stockholders and taxed again under the City's personal income tax\n(PIT)\n\nThe Legislature and the New York City Council recognized this problem\nfor unincorporated businesses and, starting in 1997, provided a partial\ncredit against the City's PIT for unincorporated business tax filers.\n\nIn 2013, the Legislature, at the request of the City Council, extended\nsimilar relief from double taxation to S-Corporations, by enacting the\ncurrent credit, applying it from January 1, 2014 through June 30, 2015.\n\nThis bill would help relieve the double taxation of owners of S-Corpora-\ntions in New York City.\n\nResident shareholders of S-Corporations would be able to take a nonre-\nfundable credit against their New York City personal income tax liabil-\nity equal to a portion of their share of the business's corporate tax\nliability. There are some similarities between this credit and the PIT\ncredit for unincorporated business taxes. One difference, however, is\nthat the S-Corporation credit is not available for shareholders with\ntaxable incomes of over $100,000, which targets the credit to middle\nincome households. There are 142,000 taxpayers that are S-Corporations\nin New York City, roughly half of all GCT taxpayers.{2} Most S-Corpora-\ntions are small businesses.\n\nFISCAL IMPACT:\n\nThis credit would reduce the personal income tax of New York City resi-\ndents who are shareholders in S-Corporations by $30 million in City\nfiscal year 2016. Extension of the credit is assumed in the City's\nfiscal year 2016 Preliminary Budget Plan. There would be no fiscal\nimpact on New York State.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately 2 New York City Department of\nFinance, tax year 2010.\n\n{2} New York City Department of Finance, tax year 2010.\n\n",
